Radiation-Induced Pneumonitis and Fibrosis
Haematology & Oncology
Dyspnoea following thoracic radiotherapy, Dry hacking cough, Inspiratory crackles over irradiated field, Geometric opacities on chest CT

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: HMO-041
Reason for Visit: Acute escalation of severe dyspnoea and intractable dry coughing in a patient recently treated with thoracic radiotherapy, requiring urgent assessment to rule out acute radiation pneumonitis versus atypical infection.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified an oncology patient 3 months post-radiation who developed a sudden, unremitting hacking cough and severe chest tightness preventing them from lying down or sleeping. Urgent in-person evaluation was required to assess respiratory reserve and initiate potent anti-inflammatory therapy, preventing progressive hypoxaemia and respiratory failure prior to the morning.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I cannot catch my breath when I lie down, my chest feels like it is locked in a vice, and I have been hacking completely dryly for twelve hours."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports an insidious onset of progressive exertional dyspnoea beginning approximately two weeks ago, exactly 10 weeks following the completion of curative thoracic radiotherapy for lung cancer. Over the last 12 hours, the dyspnoea escalated sharply, accompanied by a dry, severe, and hacking non-productive cough and a sensation of extreme chest tightness. They report low-grade fevers and generalized systemic malaise developing over the same timeline. They strictly deny any purulent sputum or haemoptysis.
Functional Status: Restricted to sitting upright in a chair; severely limited by dyspnoea on minimal exertion.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; exhaustion from continuous coughing and lack of sleep.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 | HR: 105 | Temp: 37.6 | RR: 24 | SpO2: 92 percent | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Fatigued, tachypnoeic adult. Speaking in short, broken sentences interrupted by dry coughing fits.
Physical Exam:
Respiratory: Increased work of breathing with prominent use of accessory muscles. Auscultation reveals distinct, localized, fine inspiratory crackles strictly confined to the right upper lobe corresponding exactly to the previously irradiated lung field. The contralateral lung is entirely clear. No wheeze.
Cardiovascular: Tachycardic, regular rhythm. No murmurs or JVP elevation.
Skin: Faint hyperpigmentation and radiation dermatitis visible over the right anterior chest wall.
Environmental Observations: Multiple empty water glasses near the chair used in attempts to suppress the cough.
Medication Reconciliation: Salbutamol inhaler used with absolutely zero efficacy.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Radiation-Induced Pneumonitis - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Bacterial Pneumonia - Ruled out/unlikely due to: the complete absence of purulent sputum, the strictly localized nature of the crackles conforming to a radiation port rather than a lobar anatomic boundary, and the timeline following radiotherapy.

Acute Pulmonary Embolism - Ruled out/unlikely due to: the insidious, progressive development of the cough and fever over weeks rather than a sudden, unprovoked pleuritic event, though the hypoxia warrants close monitoring.

Heart Failure - Ruled out/unlikely due to: the unilateral nature of the crackles, absence of peripheral oedema, and lack of an S3 gallop.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient's presentation of a dry hacking cough, low-grade fever, and localized crackles strictly matching a recent thoracic radiation field is pathognomonic for acute radiation pneumonitis. The objective finding of resting hypoxia and tachypnoea indicates significant alveolar inflammation and impaired gas exchange. Delaying medical intervention until normal daytime hours would expose the patient to progressive, life-threatening hypoxaemia, uncontrolled severe coughing, and potential progression to irreversible pulmonary fibrosis. This acute respiratory decompensation definitively satisfies the criteria for an urgent after-hours home visit to institute high-dose systemic corticosteroids immediately.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered immediate oral prednisone 50mg to suppress the acute radiation-induced inflammatory cascade. Arranged urgent outpatient high-resolution chest CT anticipating distinct geometric ground-glass opacities matching the radiation port, and rapid-access respiratory oncology review.
Positive Behavioral Change:

Remain seated completely upright in the chair for the remainder of the night. By keeping the torso vertical, you reduce the mechanical pressure of the abdominal organs on your diaphragm, maximizing the expansion of the unaffected lung tissue.

Utilize a cool mist humidifier in the room if available. By increasing the ambient moisture, you soothe the irritated upper airways and mechanically reduce the trigger threshold for the dry hacking cough.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review via telehealth tomorrow morning to confirm improvement in oxygen saturation.

